WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.598
This repository's check passes.

2
00:00:01.598 --> 00:00:10.390
It also runs zero tests, because the glob says dot test dot mjs and the files are named dot spec dot mjs.

3
00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:13.187
The exit code says everything is fine.

4
00:00:13.187 --> 00:00:17.183
Hand that to a repair agent and it reports success.

5
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:18.781
The transcript looks perfect.

6
00:00:18.782 --> 00:00:23.178
That is the baseline, and it is not a straw man.

7
00:00:23.178 --> 00:00:27.174
Same model, same four tools, same budget as everything below.

8
00:00:27.174 --> 00:00:33.168
The only thing it lacks is any way to find out whether it was right.

9
00:00:33.168 --> 00:00:36.053
Only repo is copied into the sandbox.

10
00:00:36.053 --> 00:00:40.586
The oracle sits beside it and the agent never sees it.

11
00:00:40.586 --> 00:00:50.889
It runs afterwards, in a different container, read-only, against a fresh copy of the repaired tree, and it does not ask for an exit code.

12
00:00:50.889 --> 00:00:53.774
It counts how many tests actually ran.

13
00:00:53.774 --> 00:00:58.719
An agent that can see the test can make the test pass.

14
00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:02.016
This is the cheapest way to stop that.

15
00:01:02.016 --> 00:01:05.381
The repository is copied and checksummed first.

16
00:01:05.381 --> 00:01:14.515
Everything after runs with no network, no capabilities, a read-only root, and the workspace copy as its only mount.

17
00:01:14.515 --> 00:01:15.957
One file changed.

18
00:01:15.958 --> 00:01:19.804
main pointed at dist/main.js; the build emits dist/index.js.

19
00:01:19.804 --> 00:01:22.208
Then the part that counts.

20
00:01:22.208 --> 00:01:31.342
The oracle imported the package through whatever entry point the manifest now declares, called the function, checked the string.

21
00:01:31.342 --> 00:01:32.304
Exit zero.

22
00:01:32.303 --> 00:01:36.630
These two checksums are the repository before and after.

23
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.957
They match, so nothing was written to your code.

24
00:01:40.957 --> 00:01:44.803
And applying prints the whole diff and waits.

25
00:01:44.803 --> 00:01:48.168
Type anything but apply and nothing happens.

26
00:01:48.168 --> 00:01:50.393
A hundred and forty runs.

27
00:01:50.393 --> 00:01:53.508
Ten broken repositories, both modes, seven repeats.

28
00:01:53.508 --> 00:01:57.513
Baseline verified forty-two of seventy, advanced fifty-one of seventy.

29
00:01:57.514 --> 00:02:06.415
A difference of twelve point nine points, ninety-five per cent interval minus two point eight to plus twenty-seven point six.

30
00:02:06.414 --> 00:02:07.749
It includes zero.

31
00:02:07.749 --> 00:02:18.875
I wrote the sample size and the rule down before the batch and pushed them first, and by that rule this is a null result.

32
00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:25.550
Seventy runs per mode do not establish that the structure is what produced the gap.

33
00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:30.000
That is not a finding that they are the same.

34
00:02:30.001 --> 00:02:34.896
What I cannot tell you is that the improvement is real.

35
00:02:34.896 --> 00:02:38.015
Seven iterations, each with the run that caused it.

36
00:02:38.015 --> 00:02:43.907
An agent that went blind to its budget and had a correct patch refused on call thirteen.

37
00:02:43.908 --> 00:02:49.454
A file reader that showed it four per cent of the file the fault was in.

38
00:02:49.454 --> 00:02:50.147
Then this.

39
00:02:50.147 --> 00:02:54.306
The baseline arm has not changed by a character across three batches.

40
00:02:54.306 --> 00:02:58.119
Fifty-three point three per cent, then forty-six point seven, then sixty.

41
00:02:58.119 --> 00:03:02.278
A spread of thirteen point three points, at temperature zero, from nothing.

42
00:03:02.278 --> 00:03:05.397
The effect I was measuring is twelve point nine.

43
00:03:05.398 --> 00:03:07.824
The noise is bigger than the signal.

44
00:03:07.824 --> 00:03:10.847
So I removed one ingredient and measured what broke.

45
00:03:10.847 --> 00:03:19.579
The bounded retry: when the harness's own check or the hidden oracle rejects the first patch, the agent gets one more turn, with the evidence attached.

46
00:03:19.578 --> 00:03:23.608
Without it, advanced goes from thirteen of thirty-five to one of thirty-five.

47
00:03:23.608 --> 00:03:26.631
Thirty-four point three points, plus sixteen to plus fifty-one.

48
00:03:26.631 --> 00:03:27.639
Clear of zero.

49
00:03:27.638 --> 00:03:30.325
But the mechanism was not what I predicted.

50
00:03:30.325 --> 00:03:35.027
Eighteen of those runs had a patch refused for running out of tool calls.

51
00:03:35.027 --> 00:03:38.385
The reserved call was also making the agent patch earlier.

52
00:03:38.385 --> 00:03:40.736
Two jobs, and I knew about one.

53
00:03:40.736 --> 00:03:45.438
So I ran it again, holding the reservation still and removing only the turn.

54
00:03:45.438 --> 00:03:48.461
Twenty-five point seven points, plus three to plus forty-five.

55
00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:49.804
Still clear of zero.

56
00:03:49.804 --> 00:03:57.864
The second attempt earns its place on its own, and it exists only because something outside the agent said the first one was wrong.

57
00:03:57.864 --> 00:04:01.473
A critic that reviews the patch before the retry decision.

58
00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:04.360
I wrote the keep rule before running it.

59
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.774
It scored one of nine against four of nine, twice, and the rule discarded it.

60
00:04:09.774 --> 00:04:11.939
And one I nearly got wrong.

61
00:04:11.939 --> 00:04:20.962
In the first batch, five repositories looked like the whole story: baseline zero out of fifteen, advanced six, a forty point gap clear of zero.

62
00:04:20.962 --> 00:04:29.263
I found that after seeing the results, so I froze those cases and made it the next batch's hypothesis instead of publishing it.

63
00:04:29.263 --> 00:04:34.316
In that batch baseline scored eleven out of thirty-five on the same five cases.

64
00:04:34.315 --> 00:04:36.120
The forty points was noise.

65
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:39.838
Every run behind every number I just said is committed.

66
00:04:39.838 --> 00:04:47.645
This puts all of them back through the same scoring code, recomputes every check, and reports anything that comes out differently.

67
00:04:47.645 --> 00:04:49.876
No key, no model, no network.

68
00:04:49.875 --> 00:04:50.990
Ten seconds, nothing.

69
00:04:50.991 --> 00:04:53.593
You do not have to believe me.

70
00:04:53.593 --> 00:04:55.080
That was the point.
